    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test \u2014 `AcceptanceTest` is the only test class configured to run).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the structured audit event alongside the existing audit line.\n\n**New file** \u2014 `OwnerCreatedEvent.java`: an immutable Java `record` (`seq`, `ownerId`, `customerCode`, `membershipLevel`, `event`). A convenience constructor pins `event` to `\"OWNER_CREATED\"`, and `toJson()` renders a compact single-line JSON object. Being a record, once emitted the event's fields cannot change.\n\n**Modified** \u2014 `AuditOwnerCreated.java`:\n- Keeps the human-readable audit line.\n- Adds a `static AtomicLong SEQ` stamped onto each event, giving a **monotonically increasing integer across all creates**.\n- After the audit line, emits `event.toJson()` to the `AUDIT` logger, e.g.\n  `{\"seq\":1,\"ownerId\":11,\"customerCode\":\"NSW-16E6609E\",\"membershipLevel\":1,\"event\":\"OWNER_CREATED\"}`.\n- The identifier is resolved through a single `primaryIdentifier(Owner)` method that today returns `customerCode`. This isolates the forward-looking requirement: when the customerCode is unified into the memberId, only that one method changes and the event automatically carries the memberId instead.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreEventCarriesIdAndIdentifier` \u2014 which asserts one `AUDIT` line contains `OWNER_CREATED`, the owner id, and the current `customerCode` \u2014 passes.",
